      },
      "note": "THE WATER TOWER'S GROUNDS, built by T-3689 for the 1871 scene: the open, planted ground the tower stood on, west of Pine Street between Chicago Avenue and Pearson Street. Kurz's lithograph of the works (Jevne & Almini, 1866-67) shows the tower alone on open ground, a lawn with shrub beds before it and young trees to its west, walks and kerbs along Chicago Avenue and Pine Street, and no house between the tower and Chicago Avenue. Greene's stereograph of the tower after the fire shows an iron railing on a stone kerb across its west side, and a photograph of about 1890 shows the same grounds as a fenced lawn. The grounds are laid inside today's Water Tower park (between Chicago Avenue, Pearson Street, Michigan Avenue and the lane on its west side); that the park keeps the city's 1860s lot is inferred, not documented."
     },
